    {
        "id": "4761fea00c851552",
        "type": "comment",
        "z": "a65f1d125d17f35a",
        "name": "Prerequisites -  Open me up and do this stuff or it won't work- Update LB9KJ/VU3ESV",
        "info": "# Launch Bitvise, Putty, or whatever your favorite shell application is\n# shell into your Raspberry Pi that is running Node-Red\n# 25-04- 2025: LB9KJ/VU3ESV: Install the following nodes before deployment\n#    node-red-dashboard\n#    node-red-contrib-ui-level\n\n# change into the hidden node-red directory\ncd ~/.node-red\n\n# add dependencies\nsudo apt-get update\nsudo apt-get install libudev-dev librtlsdr-dev libusb-1.0-0-dev libpthread-stubs0-dev git\n\n# install the usbhid Nodes.  Use this branch, not the one that comes up\n# in the Palette search.  That one won't install correctly.\n# 25-04- 2025: LB9KJ/VU3ESV: robertsLando/node-red-contrib-usbhid will not work in NodeRed 4.0\n# try the next one instead\n# sudo npm install robertsLando/node-red-contrib-usbhid\nsudo npm install somebuddy87/node-red-contrib-usbhid \n\n# 25-04- 2025: LB9KJ/VU3ESV: Install this to bring the stoptimer3\nsudo npm install zybron/node-red-contrib-stoptimer3\n# 25-04- 2025: LB9KJ/VU3ESV: Reboot the Pi\nsudo reboot now\n# 25-04- 2025: wait for the pi to reboot \ncd ~/.node-red\n\n# create a udev rule file  \nsudo nano /etc/udev/rules.d/10-telepost.rules\n\n# put this line in it and save the file (Ctrl-X then Y to save an exit Nano)\nKERNEL==\"hidraw*\", SUBSYSTEM==\"hidraw\", MODE:=\"777\", GROUP=\"telepost\"\n\n# then reload udev rules\nsudo udevadm control --reload-rules\n\n# add a new user group called telepost, and add the current user into it\n# the user needs to be the one that Node-Red is running under. \n# You likely are running Node-Red as the \"pi\" user.  \nsudo groupadd -f telepost\nsudo gpasswd -a $USER telepost\n\n\n# This must be done after above is done.\n# Turn on the LP-500 / LP-700 meter.\n\n# You will need a USB A to USB B cable.\n\n# Plug the LP-500 / LP700 USB port into a free USB port on the Pi. \n# If it was already plugged in, remove it and plug in again.\n\n# In the Flow, click the \"Pole Devices\" Inject node trigger button. \n# It triggers the getHIDdevices node. In the Debug TAB, expand the debug object.\n\n#  You should get an array of at least element.  Open it up and copy the value in \n# \"path\" to the clipboard.\n# in my case it is:  /dev/hidraw0\n\n# Edit the \"HID-LP\" (HIDdevice) node to set the path to the value found above.\n# Restart Flow, and it should be decorated with \"connected\"  If not, check your work.\n\n# This The HIDdevice node seems to not be able to recover from disconnects and \n# power resets of the LP-500 / LP-700 meter.   Leave the Meter power on!\n\n# Buy me a beer when you see me.\n# Warren, KD4Z\n\n",
        "x": 330,
        "y": 120,
        "wires": []
    },
    {
